----------------------------------------------------

Security Rules

Never trust user_id values coming from the frontend.

Always obtain the user performing an action from the authenticated session or JWT context.

Every record created by a user must store:

created_by_user_id

----------------------------------------------------

Audit Requirements

New operational entities must include:

created_at
updated_at
created_by_user_id

Prefer soft deletion or logical closure over physical deletion.

----------------------------------------------------

Architecture Rules

Respect the existing modular architecture under app/domains.

Do not move modules unless explicitly requested.

Prefer extending existing services rather than duplicating logic.

Business logic must live in service layers, not in route handlers.

----------------------------------------------------

Migration Rules

All database changes must be implemented using Alembic migrations.

Do not alter or drop existing tables without explicit instruction.

New tables must follow the existing naming and SQLAlchemy conventions used in the project.

----------------------------------------------------

Agent Behavior Requirements

Before implementing changes:

1. Analyze the existing project structure
2. Identify where SQLAlchemy models are defined
3. Identify how migrations are organized
4. Identify how authenticated user is retrieved
5. Identify how actuaciones currently work

Then propose an incremental implementation plan.

Do not implement large architectural changes without confirming the plan first.

----------------------------------------------------

Refactor Objective

Introduce the following new capabilities without breaking the current system:

- complaint registration (denuncias)
- unified trigger queue (iniciador_ruta)
- inspection route planning (ruta_trabajo)
- group assignments for inspectors
- task execution tracking
- support for not executed work cases
- improved administrative traceability

Frontend changes will come later.
Focus first on backend models, migrations, and services.